<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<worksheet>
    <title>Section 2.1 - Vectors and Linear Combinations</title>

    <introduction>
        <p>
            Visit this
            <url href="https://gvsu.edu/s/0Je" visual="gvsu.edu/s/0Je">web site</url>
            where you find an interactive figure you can use to investigate
            linear combinations.
            We will consider the vectors
            <md>
                \vvec=\twovec21, \wvec=\twovec12
            </md>
            and consider linear combinations <m>c\vvec+d\wvec</m> (which is
            shown in red).
        </p>
    </introduction>

    <exercise workspace="1in">
        <statement>
            <p>
                The weight <m>d</m> is initially set to 0 in the interactive
                figure.
                Explain what happens as you vary <m>c</m> with <m>d=0</m>?
                How is this related to scalar multiplication?
            </p>
        </statement>

        <solution component="05-solutions">
            <p>
                The vector <m>\vvec</m> stretches (when <m>c \gt 1</m> ),
                shrinks (when <m>0 \lt c \lt 1</m> ), and flips over the origin
                (when <m>c \lt 0</m> ).
                When <m>d = 0</m>, we are getting all scalar multiples of
                <m>\vvec</m>.
            </p>
        </solution>
    </exercise>

    <exercise workspace="1in">
        <statement>
            <p>
                What is the linear combination of <m>\vvec</m> and <m>\wvec</m>
                when <m>c=1</m> and <m>d=-2</m>?
                You may find this result using the interactive figure, but you
                should also verify it by computing the linear combination.
            </p>
        </statement>

        <solution component="05-solutions">
            <p>
                We can take the following linear combination:
                <md>
                    1 \left[
    \begin{array}{r}
      2 \\
      1 \\
    \end{array}
  \right] + (-2)\left[
    \begin{array}{rr|r}
      1  \\
      2  \\
    \end{array}
  \right] = \left[
    \begin{array}{rr|r}
      2-2  \\
      1-4  \\
    \end{array}
  \right] = \left[
    \begin{array}{rr|r}
      0  \\
      -3  \\
    \end{array}
  \right]
                </md>
            </p>

            <image width="50%">
                <shortdescription>
                    Linear combination of vectors v and w.
                    to get the vector [0,-3].
                </shortdescription>
                <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="06-2-2_1">
                    <diagram dimensions="(300, 300)" margins="5">
                        <definition>
                            v=(2,1)
                        </definition>

                        <definition>
                            w=(1,2)
                        </definition>
                        <coordinates bbox="(-5,-5,5,5)">
                            <grid spacings="((-6,2,6),(-6,2,6))" stroke="#ddd"/>
                            <grid basis="(v,w)" stroke="lightgray"/>
                            <axes decorations="no"/>
                            <vector v="v" alignment="se">
                                <m>
                                    {\mathbf v}
                                </m>
                            </vector>
                            <vector v="w" alignment="nw">
                                <m>
                                    {\mathbf w}
                                </m>
                            </vector>
                            <vector v="1*v-2*w" alignment="nw" stroke="red">
                                <m>
                                    1\mathbf{v}-2\mathbf{w}
                                </m>
                            </vector>
                        </coordinates>
                    </diagram>
                </prefigure>
            </image>
        </solution>
    </exercise>

    <exercise workspace="1in">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Can the vector <m>\twovec00</m> be expressed as a linear
                combination of <m>\vvec</m> and <m>\wvec</m>?
                If so, what are weights <m>c</m> and <m>d</m>?
            </p>
        </statement>

        <solution component="05-solutions">
            <p>
                Yes!
                Let <m>c=0</m> and <m>d=0</m>.
            </p>

            <image width="50%">
                <shortdescription>
                    Linear combination of vectors v and w.
                    to get the vector [0, 0]
                </shortdescription>
                <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="06-2-2_2">
                    <diagram dimensions="(300, 300)" margins="5">
                        <definition>
                            v=(2,1)
                        </definition>

                        <definition>
                            w=(1,2)
                        </definition>
                        <coordinates bbox="(-5,-5,5,5)">
                            <grid spacings="((-6,2,6),(-6,2,6))" stroke="#ddd"/>
                            <grid basis="(v,w)" stroke="lightgray"/>
                            <axes decorations="no"/>
                            <vector v="v" alignment="se">
                                <m>
                                    {\mathbf v}
                                </m>
                            </vector>
                            <vector v="w" alignment="nw">
                                <m>
                                    {\mathbf w}
                                </m>
                            </vector>
                            <vector v="0*v+0*w" alignment="nw" stroke="red">
                                <m>
                                    0\mathbf{v}+0\mathbf{w}
                                </m>
                            </vector>
                        </coordinates>
                    </diagram>
                </prefigure>
            </image>
        </solution>
    </exercise>

    <exercise workspace="1.2in">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Use the interactive figure to determine whether the vector
                <m>\twovec{-3}{0}</m> can be expressed as a linear combination
                of <m>\vvec</m> and <m>\wvec</m>?
                If so, what are weights <m>c</m> and <m>d</m>?
                How could you use the grey diamond-like background grid to
                determine this?
            </p>
        </statement>

        <solution component="05-solutions">
            <p>
                Yes!
                Let <m>c=-2</m> and <m>d=1</m>.
            </p>

            <image width="50%">
                <shortdescription>
                    Linear combination of vectors v and w.
                    to get the vector [-3,0].
                </shortdescription>
                <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="06-2-2_3">
                    <diagram dimensions="(300, 300)" margins="5">
                        <definition>
                            v=(2,1)
                        </definition>

                        <definition>
                            w=(1,2)
                        </definition>
                        <coordinates bbox="(-5,-5,5,5)">
                            <grid spacings="((-6,2,6),(-6,2,6))" stroke="#ddd"/>
                            <grid basis="(v,w)" stroke="lightgray"/>
                            <axes decorations="no"/>
                            <vector v="v" alignment="se">
                                <m>
                                    {\mathbf v}
                                </m>
                            </vector>
                            <vector v="w" alignment="nw">
                                <m>
                                    {\mathbf w}
                                </m>
                            </vector>
                            <vector v="-2*v+1*w" alignment="ne" stroke="red">
                                <m>
                                    -2\mathbf{v}+1\mathbf{w}
                                </m>
                            </vector>
                        </coordinates>
                    </diagram>
                </prefigure>
            </image>

            <p>
                Using the grey diamond-like background we can see that walking 1
                along <m>\wvec</m> and "backwards" 2 along <m>\vvec</m> gives
                the desired vector <m>\twovec{-3}{0}</m>.
            </p>
        </solution>
    </exercise>

    <exercise workspace="1.2in">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Verify the result from the previous part by algebraically
                substituting the weights <m>c</m> and <m>d</m> and the vectors
                <m>\vvec</m> and <m>\wvec</m> and checking you get
                <m>\twovec{-3}{0}</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>

        <solution component="05-solutions">
            <p>
                We can take the following linear combination:
                <md>
                    (-2) \left[
    \begin{array}{r}
      2 \\
      1 \\
    \end{array}
  \right] + (1)\left[
    \begin{array}{rr|r}
      1  \\
      2  \\
    \end{array}
  \right] = \left[
    \begin{array}{rr|r}
      -4+1  \\
      -2+2  \\
    \end{array}
  \right] = \left[
    \begin{array}{rr|r}
      -3  \\
      0  \\
    \end{array}
  \right]
                </md>
            </p>
        </solution>
    </exercise>

    <exercise workspace="1.2in">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Can the vector <m>\twovec{1.3}{-1.7}</m> be expressed as a
                linear combination of <m>\vvec</m> and <m>\wvec</m>?
                (Use the diagram, how to do this algebraically is on the next
                page.)
            </p>
        </statement>

        <solution component="05-solutions">
            <p>
                We can travel in directions parallel to <m>\vvec</m> and
                <m>\wvec</m>.
                We get roughly there by going about 1.3 in the direction of
                <m>\vvec</m> and -1.3 in the direction of <m>\wvec</m>.
            </p>

            <image width="50%">
                <shortdescription>
                    Linear combination of vectors v and w to get (approximately) the vector [1.3, -1.7].
                </shortdescription>
                <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="06-2-2_4">
                    <diagram dimensions="(300, 300)" margins="5">
                        <definition>
                            v=(2,1)
                        </definition>

                        <definition>
                            w=(1,2)
                        </definition>
                        <coordinates bbox="(-5,-5,5,5)">
                            <grid spacings="((-6,2,6),(-6,2,6))" stroke="#ddd"/>
                            <grid basis="(v,w)" stroke="lightgray"/>
                            <axes decorations="no"/>
                            <vector v="v" alignment="se">
                                <m>
                                    {\mathbf v}
                                </m>
                            </vector>
                            <vector v="w" alignment="nw">
                                <m>
                                    {\mathbf w}
                                </m>
                            </vector>
                            <vector v="1.3*v-1.3*w" alignment="ne" stroke="red">
                                <m>
                                    1.3\mathbf{v}-1.3\mathbf{w}
                                </m>
                            </vector>
                        </coordinates>
                    </diagram>
                </prefigure>
            </image>
        </solution>
    </exercise>

    <exercise workspace="1in">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Can you find any two-dimensional vectors that cannot be
                expressed as linear combinations of <m>\vvec</m> and
                <m>\wvec</m>?
            </p>
        </statement>

        <solution component="05-solutions">
            <p>
                No!
                We can travel along the gray diamond-like grid lines (sometimes
                partially so) to get anywhere in the <m>xy</m>-plane.
            </p>
        </solution>
    </exercise>

    <outcomes>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                Make sure you can answer the following questions:
            </p>
        </introduction>

        <ul>
            <li>
                <p>
                    How do we multiply a vector by a scalar?
                    How do we add two vectors?
                </p>
            </li>

            <li>
                <p>
                    How do we picture vector addition and scalar multiplication
                    geometrically?
                </p>
            </li>

            <li>
                <p>
                    What is a linear combination of vectors?
                </p>
            </li>
        </ul>
    </outcomes>
</worksheet>